{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 12, "model_name": "meta/llama-3.3-70b-instruct", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a Count of Tripoli from 1152 to 1187 he was the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem he succeeded his father as Count of Tripoli after his death in 1152. \nThe *Early Life* section of his biography states that Raymond III was born around 1140 to Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem his mother was the daughter of King Baldwin II of Jerusalem and his wife Morphia of Melitene. \nRaymond III played a significant role in the *Military Campaigns* section of his life he was a skilled military leader and led several campaigns against the Muslim forces in the region he was also involved in the Battle of Inab in 1149 where he fought alongside King Baldwin III of Jerusalem. \nThe *Regency* section of his life is also significant Raymond III was appointed as regent for King Baldwin IV of Jerusalem in 1174 after the king fell ill he held this position until 1177 when he was replaced by Guy of Lusignan. \nRaymond III was also involved in the *Battle of Hattin* section of his life where he fought against Saladin in 1187 the battle was a significant defeat for the Crusader forces and Raymond III was forced to flee to the city of Tripoli. \nThe *Later Life* section of his biography states that Raymond III died on September 1187 after the Battle of Hattin his death marked the end of the County of Tripoli as a major power in the region. \nRaymond III was also known for his *Diplomatic Efforts* section of his life where he attempted to form alliances with other Christian leaders in the region he was also involved in the *Siege of Kerak* section of his life where he fought against Saladin in 1183. \nThe *Legacy* section of his biography states that Raymond III was remembered as a skilled military leader and a strong advocate for the Christian cause in the region he played a significant role in the 